================ N-BIT LADDER, 640 x 480 ================
 Is the N-bit path LINEAR in N (2N stages) or exponential (2*(2^N - 1))?
 Every row is checked against cv::filter2D on the same values first, and
 cv::filter2D is then TIMED on those same values -- so N >= 2, which is what
 every pyramid level above 0 runs, has a real denominator and not only an
 oracle. The denominator needs no scale factor here: the CV_8U image holds
 the pixel VALUES, not {0, 255}.

 N    binCV ns/px OpenCV ns/px  vs OpenCV   vs N=1  binCV bytes  OpenCV bytes    2N stg  replicated
 ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 1        0.04808     0.50905     10.59x    1.00x     192000 B     1536000 B         2           2
 2        0.11081     0.51543      4.65x    2.30x     307200 B     1536000 B         4           6
 3        0.20494     0.52451      2.56x    4.26x     422400 B     1536000 B         6          14
 4        0.30267     0.51366      1.70x    6.30x     537600 B     1536000 B         8          30
 5        0.38204     0.51325      1.34x    7.95x     652800 B     1536000 B        10          62

 'vs N=1' against the STAGES column is the whole reading: a cost that tracks
 2N is the linear formulation, one that tracks the replicated column is the
 exponential one this was written to avoid. The two axes together write
 2*(N+1) destination planes at row N, so part of any growth is stores.
 READ THE TWO BYTE COLUMNS WITH THE RATIO, per CLAUDE.md. binCV's footprint
 is the column that MOVES: 3(N+1) - 1 bit-planes against OpenCV's flat one
 byte-plane plus two 16-bit planes. binCV stays smaller at every N in this
 ladder, but the margin narrows as N rises, and the crossing point is what
 (bits per pyramid level) is for -- it is not assumed here.
 Note the fixed per-call cost printed above applies to the OpenCV column at
 every row of this ladder too.

### run 7
 binarized spatial derivative -- vs cv::filter2D with the same kernel
================================================================================

OpenCV 4.8.0-dev, cv::getNumThreads() = 1; binCV is single-threaded

DENOMINATOR: the reference pipeline's calcBinarizedDeriv on the SAME binary
content stored as CV_8U -- two cv::filter2D calls with [-1, 0, 1] as a 1x3 and
a 3x1, ported. That is what the pipeline runs today without binCV.

The DENOMINATOR row is 'OpenCV filter2D x2' -- the derivative and nothing else.
'OpenCV as-written' adds the reference's own `*= 16` and cv::merge, which binCV
reproduces neither of (ops/derivative.hpp: the scale is representational, and
interleaved channels are unreachable to a word-parallel popcount). Charging
those to the baseline would flatter binCV, so they are printed and not used.

Every row computes BOTH AXES, because that is what a VIO frontend needs before
it can form the covariance.

The checksum column folds every destination pixel's VALUE and is
representation-independent, so all rows of a size must print the same number.

READ THE WORKING-SET COLUMN WITH THE RATIO. binCV's whole working set is five
BIT-planes where OpenCV's is one byte-plane and two 16-bit planes -- 8x smaller
-- so on a 1 MiB-L2 Cortex-A part of any large ratio at 640x480 is residency
rather than arithmetic. The size ladder is
there to separate the two: if the ratio collapses once BOTH sides fit in cache,
the headline figure was residency; if it holds, it is the operation.

FIXED PER-CALL COST (hazard 5), measured on a 2x2 frame -- the part of every
row below that is not the operation:
 OpenCV, 2 x cv::filter2D:    1.856 us per call
 binCV, derivativeX + derivativeY:    0.012 us per call
 Divided by a frame's pixel count this is what a small frame's ns/pixel is
 mostly made of. It is NOT subtracted from the tables; it is printed beside
 them so the ladder is read with it.

